questions.explanation:
We have the following events: [latex]A[/latex] : student does well in maths [latex]B[/latex] : student does well in physics [latex]P(A^{c})[/latex] : student doesn't do well in maths [latex]P(B^{c})[/latex] : student doesn't do well in physics [latex]P(A^{c} \cap B^{c})[/latex] : student doesn't do well both maths and physics Using DeMorgan's laws, we have: [latex]
A^{c} \cap B^{c} = (A \cup B)^{c}
[/latex] This gives us: [latex]
\begin{aligned}
P(A^{c} \cap B^{c}) &= P(A \cup B)^{c}\\
&= 1 - P(A \cup B)\\
\end{aligned}
[/latex] We can now compute [latex]P(A \cup B)[/latex] : [latex]
\begin{aligned}
P(A \cup B) &= P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)\\
&= 0.6 + 0.7 - 0.5\\
&= 0.8
\end{aligned}
[/latex] The required probability is therefore [latex]{0.2}[/latex] .

