If [latex]f[/latex] is odd, then [latex]f(-x) = -f(x)[/latex]. Then: [latex]
\begin{aligned}
g(-x) &= f(-x) - 2\\
&= -f(x) - 2\\
&= -[f(x) + 2]
\end{aligned}
[/latex] This doesn't allow us to conclude anything about the nature of [latex]g[/latex]. A simple counter-example will show that if [latex]f[/latex] is odd, [latex]g[/latex] need not be odd. For instance, if [latex]f(x)= x[/latex], we have [latex]g(x) = x - 2[/latex], which is neither odd nor even. If [latex]f[/latex] is even, then [latex]f(-x) = f(x)[/latex]. Then: [latex]
\begin{aligned}
g(-x) &= f(-x) - 2\\
&= f(x) - 2\\
&= g(x)
\end{aligned}
[/latex] Therefore, if [latex]f[/latex] is even, then [latex]g[/latex] is also even. A similar argument holds for the case when [latex]g[/latex] is even.

