questions.explanation:
Matrix multiplication doesn't increase the rank. To see why note that we can view matrix multiplication in the following way. If [latex]B = \begin{bmatrix}b_1 & \cdots & b_n\end{bmatrix}[/latex] has [latex]n[/latex] columns, then [latex]AB[/latex] can be written as: [latex]
AB = \begin{bmatrix}
Ab_1 & \cdots & Ab_n
\end{bmatrix}
[/latex] We see that the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] column of [latex]AB[/latex] is a linear combination of the columns of [latex]A[/latex] , with the multipliers coming from the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] column of [latex]b[/latex]. Thus the column space of [latex]AB[/latex] is a subspace of the column space of [latex]A[/latex]. It follows that [latex]\text{rank}(AB) \leqslant \text{rank}(A)[/latex]. We could also view matrix multiplication in another way. If [latex]A = \begin{bmatrix}r_1^T\\\vdots\\r_m^T\end{bmatrix}[/latex] has [latex]m[/latex] rows, then [latex]AB[/latex] can be written as: [latex]
AB = \begin{bmatrix}
r_1^TB\\
\vdots\\
r_m^TB
\end{bmatrix}
[/latex] We see that the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] row of [latex]AB[/latex] is a linear combination of the rows of [latex]B[/latex] , with the multipliers coming form the [latex]i^{th}[/latex] row of [latex]A[/latex]. Thus the row space of [latex]AB[/latex] is a subspace of the row space of [latex]B[/latex]. It follows that [latex]\text{rank}(AB) \leqslant \text{rank}(B)[/latex]. Combining the two inequalities, we have: [latex]
\text{rank}(AB) \leqslant \min(\text{rank}(A), \text{rank}(B))
[/latex] Thus if we start with a matrix [latex]A[/latex] and multiply it by some matrix [latex]B[/latex] , the rank of the resulting matrix cannot be more than the rank of [latex]A[/latex].

