[latex] P[/latex] is a projection transformation for [latex] \mathbb{R}^{n}[/latex] onto the vector [latex] v=( v_{1} ,\cdots ,v_{n})[/latex]. Then for any [latex] x\in \mathbb{R}^{n}[/latex] : [latex]
\begin{aligned}
P( x) & =\frac{x^{T} v}{v^{T} v} v
\end{aligned}
[/latex] The matrix corresponding to this transformation is given by: [latex]
P=\begin{bmatrix}
| &  & |\\
P( e_{1}) & \cdots  & P( e_{n})\\
| &  & |
\end{bmatrix} =\frac{1}{v^{T} v}\begin{bmatrix}
| &  & |\\
v_{1} v & \cdots  & v_{n} v\\
| &  & |
\end{bmatrix} =\frac{1}{v^{T} v} vv^{T}
[/latex] We have [latex] P_{ii} =\frac{v_{i}^{2}}{v^{T} v}[/latex]. Summing this from [latex] i=1[/latex] to [latex] i=n[/latex] , we get the trace as [latex] 1[/latex].

