For a matrix to be the covariance matrix of some dataset, it has to be symmetric positive semi-definite. Consider a symmetric matrix: [latex]

C=\begin{bmatrix}
d_{1} & a\\
a & d_{2}
\end{bmatrix}

[/latex] For [latex] C[/latex] to be positive semi-definite, its eigenvalues have to be non-negative. The characteristic polynomial is: [latex]

( d_{1} -\lambda )( d_{2} -\lambda ) -a^{2} =\lambda ^{2} -( d_{1} +d_{2}) \lambda +\left( d_{1} d_{2} -a^{2}\right)

[/latex] For the eigenvalues to be non-negative, we see that: [latex] d_{1} +d_{2} \geqslant 0[/latex] and [latex] d_{1} d_{2} -a^{2} \geqslant 0[/latex] From this, we conclude that both [latex] d_{1}[/latex] and [latex] d_{2}[/latex] have to be non-negative, with [latex] |a|\leqslant \sqrt{d_{1} d_{2}}[/latex]. From the matrices given in the option, we see that (1) and (4) satisfy these conditions.

